iting a long time well  maybe there's there's uh other newer  licensing  options as well that become  more prominent or recognized as sort of  being the need to support the open  source world i know there's some  innovation and  a bunch of people thinking about that as  well  awesome wes well um thank you for for  joining us  um just in terms of a final question um  since our time is up i just wanted to  ask you what's the most surprising thing  you've learned  in the last few years especially  building arrow  the most surprising thing um  [Laughter]  um i i will say like around i think i  think one of the  things i that i didn't understand as  well  um a few years ago was that  i kind of around the open source  economics question  um was that probably the biggest barrier  to corporations funding open source is  the way that they budget  and so because there is not a clear like  a clean  line item in people's budgets for  sponsoring open source  that makes it difficult for um  for you know whoever is uh you know  responsible for  or interested in sponsoring a project  they don't have like an obvious place  like you know to to put it whereas  like you know people have huge budgets  to pay for computers or to pay for cloud  computing  or to pay for enterprise software but  there's no like  you know people are not yet in a point  where they are out you know they're  um you know they're reserving you know  even one-tenth of one percent of their  i.t budget to fund  to sponsor open source now and so it  wasn't  as apparent to me that that was a  problem until i started going out to  uh to look for sponsorships um and then  people would say oh well you know we  don't know where the money's going to  come from  and then you say well you're a very rich  company you know  clearly you know you find enough money  between the seat cushions but it's  it turns out it's not that simple  well that's well said and for those of  us who are starting companies or  um or who have manage